'''''Cotoneaster integerrimus''''', the '''common cotoneaster''', is a species of ''[[Cotoneaster]]'' native to central and eastern [[Europe]] and southwest [[Asia]], from southern [[Belgium]] and eastern [[France]] south to [[Italy]], and east through [[Germany]] to the [[Balkans]], northern [[Turkey]], the [[Crimea]], the [[Caucasus]] and northern [[Iran]]; plants in [[Spain]] may also belong in this species. In the past, it was treated in a wider sense, including plants from [[Wales]] now split off as ''[[Cotoneaster cambricus]]'' and plants from [[Scandinavia]] now treated as ''[[Cotoneaster scandinavicus]]'', but differs from these in genetic profile and detail of foliage and fruit.<ref name=sap>{{cite web|url=http://www.conwy.gov.uk/upload/public/attachments/48/WildCotoneasterv2.pdf|title=Wild Cotoneaster ''Cotoneaster cambricus''|publisher=Species Action Plan|format=[[Portable Document Format|PDF]]|accessdate=August 21, 2007|url-status=dead|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20070927064747/http://www.conwy.gov.uk/upload/public/attachments/48/WildCotoneasterv2.pdf|archivedate=September 27, 2007}}</ref>

It is a [[deciduous]] [[shrub]] growing to {{convert|2|m}} tall. The [[leaf|leaves]] are oval to oval-acute, {{convert|1|–|4|cm}} long, green and thinly pubescent above at first, later glabrous, and densely pubescent below and on the leaf margin, with pale grey hairs. The [[flower]]s are produced in [[corymb]]s of one to four (occasionally up to seven) together in mid spring, each flower {{convert|3|mm}} diameter, with five white to pale pink petals. The [[fruit]] is a dark red [[pome]] {{convert|6|–|8|mm}} diameter, containing two or three [[seed]]s, averaging 2.8. Fruits average 85.3% water.{{sfn|Ehrlén|Eriksson|1991}} It occurs on [[limestone]] soils, at altitudes of up to {{convert|2800|m}} altitude.<ref name=blamey>{{cite book|author1=Blamey, M.  |author2=Grey-Wilson, C.|year=1989|title=''Flora of Britain and Northern Europe''|isbn=0-340-40170-2}}</ref><ref name=rhs>{{cite book|author=Huxley, A.|year=1992|title=''New RHS Dictionary of Gardening''|publisher= Macmillan|isbn=0-333-47494-5}}</ref>

==Nomenclature==
The genus name ''Cotoneaster'' comes from Latin ''cotone'', [[quince]], and the suffix ''-aster'', meaning ''resembling''. ''Cotone'' is a masculine noun, though in some older works it was wrongly treated as feminine, resulting in different name endings for many of the species, such as ''Cotoneaster integerrima'' instead of ''Cotoneaster integerrimus''.<ref name=bean>{{cite book|author=Bean, W. J.|year=1976|title=''Trees and Shrubs Hardy in the British Isles''|edition=8|publisher=[[John Murray (publishing house)|John Murray]]|isbn=0-7195-1790-7}}</ref> The [[International Code of Nomenclature for algae, fungi, and plants|International Code of Botanical Nomenclature]] (articles 23.5 and 32.7 in the 2007 Vienna code) specifies that such names are not invalid, but are to be corrected without altering the author or date of publication.

